{"agentDecisions":{"entries":[{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** Reviewer dispositions are `resolved`, `unsupported`,\n  `unaffected`, `present` and `present-low`; there is no `fixed`. What is a\n  \"fixed disposition\"?\n- **Answer:** The implementer's claim. `gate.execute` and `review.execute`\n  take `claims: [{findingId, claim}]`, which PROTOCOL.md describes as\n  \"asserted fixes\" and also uses for rebuttals. Each claim gains a required\n  `disposition`: `fixed` (the implementer changed code to fix the finding) or\n  `disputed` (a rebuttal: the finding is not a defect or is intentionally\n  unchanged). A `fixed` claim requires a `sweep` record; a `disputed` claim\n  must not carry one.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** (a) a reviewer disposition `fixed` (rejected: reviewers\n  verify fixes, the implementer claims them); (b) `disposition` optional, with\n  an unlabeled claim treated as not fixed (rejected: an implementer could skip\n  the sweep by leaving the label off, which defeats AC4); (c) every claim is a\n  fix (rejected: rebuttals are claims too).\n- **Reversibility:** an input-contract change; callers add one field. A later\n  version can add more disposition values (for example for #148).","file":"interview.md","heading":"D1. Where a `fixed` disposition lives"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** What exactly does GateReeve check?\n- **Answer:** `sweep: {class, search, sites: [{path, line}], fix, test,\n  dependents}`. Every field is required. `class`, `search`, `fix`, `test` and\n  `dependents` are non-empty text of at most 4096 characters; `sites` has 1 to\n  256 entries, each a non-empty `path` (at most 1024 characters) and a\n  positive integer `line`. Unknown keys are rejected. A failure refuses the\n  execution before any reviewer starts and names the field, for example\n  `claims[0].sweep.search`. GateReeve checks presence and shape only; the\n  reviewers verify truth (D12 item 3). Site paths are not checked against the\n  candidate: a swept site may have been deleted by the fix.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** verify paths exist in the candidate (rejected: deleted\n  sites are legitimate, and truth is the reviewers' job); `search` inside each\n  site (rejected: the source plan has one `search` for the sweep).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible; bounds can be relaxed.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D2. Sweep record shape and validation"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** `class` is text. How does GateReeve match a later finding to\n  a swept class?\n- **Answer:** By the claimed finding's `classId`, taken from the retained\n  review that reported it (claims naming no retained finding are already\n  dropped, #141). The record keeps the implementer's `class` text alongside.\n  Reviewers are told to give a missed site of a swept class that record's\n  `classId`.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** the implementer supplies a `classId` (rejected: it would\n  let the implementer relabel the class reviewers named); match by class text\n  (rejected: free text does not match reliably).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible; the record carries both values.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D3. Which class a sweep record sweeps"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** \"The next round's reviewers receive the sweep records.\" Only\n  the claims of this round, or all of them?\n- **Answer:** All records of the open review scope. A boundary review's\n  packet carries `sweepRecords: {format: \"sweep-record/1\", guide, records}`:\n  the records built from this execution's `fixed` claims plus every record in\n  the inputs of earlier boundary reviews whose revision belongs to the same\n  open scope (`state.reviewScopes`). Each record carries `findingId`,\n  `classId`, `round` (the round that received the claim) and the sweep\n  fields. When a scope closes (integration, split or amendment) its records\n  stop being carried, like its round counter. The field is absent when there\n  are no records, so packets without claims are unchanged.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** only this round's claims (rejected: a round-5 reviewer\n  would not see the round-4 sweep it must hold the fix to); a new journal\n  event per record (rejected: more machinery; review inputs are already\n  retained and content-addressed).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D4. How sweep records reach the next round"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** How does \"the next round treats a missed site of a swept class\n  as a regression\" become mechanical?\n- **Answer:** When a boundary review is qualified, a finding whose `classId`\n  equals a record's `classId` (records of rounds up to the one being\n  qualified) and whose ID is not that record's claimed finding is a\n  regression of the previous fix, in addition to the existing path rule. So a\n  medium finding of a swept class keeps blocking in rounds 4-5. GateReeve\n  never sets a reviewer's `blocking` flag (correction policy): the reviewer\n  instructions and the packet guide tell reviewers that such a finding is a\n  regression and blocks in rounds 4-5. A finding raised before the sweep that\n  is still reported after it is also a missed site.\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** compare the finding's origin round with the record's round\n  (rejected: a site already known when the sweep was claimed and still present\n  afterwards is exactly a missed site).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D5. A missed site of a swept class is a regression"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** Fold in #134?\n- **Answer:** Yes. It is the same class as #144's cross-round regression\n  tracking and the same function (`findingObligations`' peer prepass). Two\n  changes: the prepass counts a peer's re-reported finding ID as still\n  present, so a peer's `resolved` cannot clear the origin; and reviews of one\n  round are grouped by the round policy's scope, round and revision (the\n  boundary review packet's `roundPolicy` gains `revision`), so two individual\n  selection-tagged reviews of one round are judged together. Inputs without a\n  revision keep the old grouping (`reviewRound`, else the result itself).\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Alternatives:** leave #134 for a separate PR (rejected: the fix sits in\n  the code this change edits and has the same tests).\n- **Reversibility:** reversible.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D6. Fold in #134 (peer re-report loses a regression's origin)"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Question:** Planning reviews, light runs, metrics?\n- **Answer:** Planning reviews take no claims, so they are unchanged. Light\n  runs use the same review path and get the same behavior. Metrics for sweep\n  records are not added (AC11's remainder is #149).\n- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Reversibility:** additive later.","file":"interview.md","heading":"D7. Scope limits"},{"answeredBy":"agent","body":"- **Answered by:** agent\n- **Decision:** `approve-design` recorded after the Codex design review\n  (round 1) PASSed with no findings. Interview D1-D7 are agent answers for\n  Trent to check.\n- **Reversibility:** a later amendment can revise any entry.","file":"scratchpad.md","heading":"S2. Design approved under autonomous-evaluator authority"}],"rule":"This is a goal run: the human was not asked. Assess every supplied agent-answered decision entry against the fixed mandate and the approved requirements. An entry that contradicts the mandate, exceeds its scope or makes an irreversible choice the human would need to make is blocking."},"artifact":"spec","contract":"gatereeve/current/8","documents":{"design.md":{"bytes":3517,"hash":"sha256:c6b4c697e9a5bdf0f9705f400b0df1778dd3a4a18d751379753aad794c147e4b","path":"design.md"},"issues.md":{"bytes":1172,"hash":"sha256:965179da239daab3c2bb2185393f33a515b667c8d28765dbd0e5003ef598a068","path":"issues.md"},"plan.md":{"bytes":1987,"hash":"sha256:d530c34b2d0ab77d4546829faf1b2208de1ef2dc0b196deeaf78b1339f5ce95d","path":"plan.md"},"spec.md":{"bytes":7942,"hash":"sha256:56644ce97b75406d490bef4402feaf1e724ec6b3dd813871e24a216e361763e6","path":"spec.md"}},"evaluator":{"contextId":"135082e9-64b5-4e14-8613-9604c4a298f2","freshContext":true,"inheritedTurns":0,"model":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"claude-code","reasoningEffort":"high"},"featureId":"tb-convergence-sweep-records","findings":[{"blocking":false,"classId":"rubric-partial-ac-coverage","classStatement":"A rubric row's pass condition tests only part of the behavior its acceptance criterion states.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC2 says \"A missing, empty, wrongly typed or out-of-bounds field ... is refused naming its path\", but R2 only tests missing, empty and over-bound text fields, empty or oversized sites, and a bad path or line. Nothing tests a wrongly typed text field (for example class: 42) or a sites value that is not a list.","failureScenario":"Implementer only checks string length -> a numeric `class` passes validation and the tests -> R2 still PASSes while part of AC2 is unverified.","id":"F1","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"undefined-dedup-identity","classStatement":"A deduplication requirement does not state what makes two items the same.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3 says records are carried \"without duplicates\" but does not say what makes two records duplicates. In a paired round, both peers' inputs hold the same records. A finding claimed fixed again in a later round produces a second record with the same findingId but a different round or content. R3 tests neither case.","failureScenario":"Implementer dedupes by findingId only -> a second sweep of the same finding in a later round is silently dropped from later packets, or no dedupe is done and paired-round records appear twice. Neither is caught by R3.","id":"F2","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"rubric-partial-ac-coverage","classStatement":"A rubric row's pass condition tests only part of the behavior its acceptance criterion states.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC6 says a peer's \"`resolved` or `unsupported` in that round does not close it\", but R6 tests only the #134 scenario, which uses `resolved`.","failureScenario":"Prepass fix handles `resolved` but not `unsupported` -> a peer's `unsupported` still clears a re-reported regression's origin -> R6 PASSes anyway.","id":"F3","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"unspecified-null-ordering","classStatement":"A nullable value is used in an ordering comparison without a defined rule for null.","document":"requirements/spec.md","evidence":"AC3 allows a record's `round` to be null \"outside the round policy\". AC4 compares records \"from a round no later than the one being qualified\" but does not say how a null round compares.","failureScenario":"A record with a null round ends up in an open scope -> the implementation treats null as 0 (or as always later) -> it inconsistently includes or excludes the record from the regression check.","id":"F4","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"stale-artifact-metadata","classStatement":"Artifact status or cross-references do not match the recorded approval state.","document":"requirements/design.md","evidence":"The design's status line still says \"**Status:** draft\", but scratchpad S2 records that `approve-design` was given. The design and spec also cite interview D1-D8, while only D1-D7 were supplied for assessment.","failureScenario":"A later reader checks the design's status -> sees draft and questions whether implementation was authorized against an approved design.","id":"F5","severity":"low"},{"blocking":false,"classId":"mandate-reporting-step-unplanned","classStatement":"A reporting duty the mandate requires has no step in the plan.","document":"requirements/plan.md","evidence":"The mandate requires open choices, including folding in #134, to be called out in the PR description. The plan's Strategy, Steps and Verification sections never mention the PR description.","failureScenario":"The PR is opened without noting that #134 was folded in, or without listing the D1-D7 choices -> the human has to rediscover them during review.","id":"F6","severity":"low"}],"kind":"planning-review","mandate":{"constraints":["Full rigor; the issue and docs/issues/tb-convergence-and-retirement/follow-up-feature.md (AC4) are the design input.","Where a choice is genuinely open, decide it on the merits, record it in the decision log and call it out in the PR description.","Verdict correction policy: GateReeve corrects only what follows from the verdict itself; meaning-changing gaps go to the repair turn.","Fold in issue #134 only if it falls naturally out of the change, and say so.","Stay mostly in round policy and disposition records; keep any edit to execution.js, boundary-review.js or local-agent-adapters.js small (parallel #142/#143 work).","Keep the disposition record shape extensible for #148 (follow-up records).","No local paths or other projects' names in public issue or PR text; avoid quoting the literal NUL escape in review text.","Do not merge."],"goal":"One PR that closes GitHub issue #144 (Convergence: class-level fix records / sweep records, AC4), including the cross-round regression tracking follow-up, taken to a ready, unmerged PR."},"mandateAssessment":{"evidence":"Goal: the spec targets #144 AC4 sweep records plus cross-round regression tracking. AC4 makes a missed site of a swept class a regression, and the plan is one slice and one PR with no merge step. #134 is folded in (D6, AC6) with a stated reason: it is the same regression-origin class and sits in the same qualification path. Round policy and disposition records: most of the work lives in the new sweep-records.js, in review-findings.js and in boundary-review.js `roundSchedule`. The execution.js edits are limited to the validation call, packet fields, roundPolicy.revision and one instruction sentence, and local-agent-adapters.js is untouched, which leaves room for the parallel #142/#143 work. Correction policy: AC4 states \"GateReeve never changes a reviewer's `blocking` flag\"; reviewers are guided, not overridden. Extensibility for #148: D1 allows more disposition values, and the design reserves new optional record fields under a new format version (`sweep-record/1`). The agent decisions D1-D7 and S2 are recorded with alternatives, can be reversed, and none of them is an irreversible choice the human would need to make. No public text is produced at this stage.","outcome":"PASS"},"outcome":"PASS","output":{"bytes":5998,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:90c04c260eaeb1ce3e1e0abc4eaa8b96fdcc4e043e88a4a8fc9af94d7a70a36b"},"receipt":{"bytes":1368,"format":"json","hash":"sha256:46cb15a9998d60360432a18b43926f9f8b9d6d0563e5d7acbe38a4cf17cfb3cd"},"reviewContract":"sha256:ead3f9c92af273e529c77b97d253cb77e89ce43e589246dc767b07f0ecee9bab","runId":"tb-convergence-sweep-records-1","subject":"sha256:2bd95bbbbe9e24798cfda1e1c35b86a1b95202c63bfd9106d5a604e6e513c7be","summary":"The spec can go to implementation; none of the findings block. Each of AC1-AC7 is observable and testable, and each matches the design's chosen shape (D1-D6): the claim disposition, the bounded sweep record with field-path errors, records carried through the open review scope, the swept-class regression rule that never changes a reviewer's blocking flag, the reviewer guide, the #134 prepass and grouping fix, and the docs plus the byte-identical mirror. Rubric rows R1-R7 each have binary pass and fail conditions and name their evidence. The plan steps P1-P6 and issues I-1 to I-4 cover every rubric row and add nothing beyond the design. All seven agent-answered decisions (D1-D7, S2) stay within the mandate, can be reversed and are recorded. The six findings are all low and non-blocking: test gaps and ambiguities at the edges, plus some housekeeping."}